==============================================================================
ATTRIBUTION of the model-minus-actual gap
==============================================================================

NTSX, 8.1 years: +0.02 pp/yr
  Sources of difference, each independently measured or documented:
    synthetic bonds sit ~+0.30pp/yr above traded UST ETFs, of which
      ~0.15pp is those ETFs' own expense ratio; on 0.6x notional the
      residual model richness is worth roughly      -0.09 pp/yr
    futures roll cost / basis, not modelled          -0.05 pp/yr (est.)
    fund securities lending income, not modelled     +0.02 pp/yr (est.)
  The residual is inside the noise of an 8-year window: the standard
  error of an annualised return at 18% vol over 8.1 years is about 6.4 pp/yr.

NTSG, 1.8 years: +0.95 pp/yr
  Largest identified sources of tracking error, in order:
    1. equity sleeve.  NTSG screens its 1,500-stock universe on ESG
       criteria; URTH does not.  The two developed-market proxies
       available here differ from each other by ~1pp/yr, which is
       larger than the whole model gap.
    2. bond currency weights are an ESTIMATE derived from the fund's
       published country weights (see ec_bonds.CCY_WEIGHTS).
    3. the exact 8 futures contracts are not published.
    4. non-synchronous closes (London vs New York) -- affects
       tracking error and daily correlation, not cumulative return.
  A 1.8-year window cannot separate these; the NTSX test is what
  carries the replication claim.

